  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "processingLevel": "major",
  "presentation": {
    "attributionShort": "Federico and Tena-Junguito; WTO",
    "topicTagsLinks": [
      "Trade & Globalization"
    ]
  },
  "descriptionKey": "- One of the defining developments of the past two centuries has been the increasing integration of national economies into a single global system. This indicator illustrates the expansion of trade and the growing interconnectedness of the world economy since 1800.\n- It shows the volume of world trade, measuring the goods exchanged across national borders at constant prices, so that changes in prices don’t distort comparisons over time. The series is expressed as an index relative to a baseline year (here, 1800 = 1).\n- The historical component is based on [Federico and Tena-Junguito (2019)](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0212610918000216), who compiled trade statistics from hundreds of historical sources, covering the main trading economies from 1800 and almost every country and colony from 1850, up to 1938, adjusting all estimates to 1913 borders. They measure world trade as the sum of all exports at constant prices, and link their series to United Nations trade statistics for 1950. There are no estimates for 1939–1949. Their original index was expressed relative to 1913 = 100; here, we have re-indexed it so that 1800 = 1.\n- Because definitions varied, the researchers standardized the historical data to modern UN conventions: smuggling, re-exports, transit trade, and bullion were excluded; fiscal years were aligned with calendar years; imports were valued including freight, and exports excluding it. Researchers used detailed price indexes to correct for inflation and shipping costs.\n- After 1950, we extend the series using World Trade Organization (WTO) data on the volume of world merchandise trade, calculated as the average of exports and imports. The WTO index was originally set to 1950 = 100, and we rescaled it to align with the historical series so that all data are expressed on the same 1800 = 1 scale. This allows us to present a consistent long-run view of world trade growth from 1800 to the present.",
